\begin{shaded}

\section*{Summary} A retrospective analysis of the Pember Fan monitoring data indicates that the species composition of the fan is changing. Grasses (both exotic and indigenous) are increasing in areal coverage while forbs are diminishing. At the same time the amount of bare ground is shrinking. There are also indications the fan is becoming more depauperate over time, with indigenous biodiversity increasing with perpendicular distance from farming operations. \emph{Brachyscome pinnata} (Nationally Critical), an indigenous forb restricted to the southern half of the fan is disappearing quickly, and extinction appears eminent if its current trajectory continues.  

Currently, there is a proposal to increase stock grazing to increase the rate of disturbance to preserve this species from being usurped by grasses. Enabling a BACI-like analysis seems unlikely going forward  as there appears no way to maintain current management independently of management change unless a high-cost fencing project is enabled in the southern portion of the fan. There is, however, potential to use the recreational reserve as a pseudo-control (or pseudo-treatment) to compare management effectiveness.


\end{shaded}


\section*{Background}

The Pember Fan is an alluvial outwash surface in Lees Valley, North Canterbury, formed from gravels deposited where the Ashley/Rakahuri River emerges into the valley. Much of the area is classified as a 'threatened land environment’. The fan supports a grassland which includes several nationally threatened and at-risk plants, most notably the nationally critical Lees Valley daisy (\emph{Brachyscome pinnata}), whose only known natural population occurs there. Other threatened species include \emph{Gingidia enysii}, \emph{Sonchus novae-zelandiae} and \emph{Raoulia monroi}.

In 2019 the Department of Conservation established monitoring programme based on 30 plots (each with 50 subplots) to monitor vegetative changes in this ecosystem through time. The underlying purpose of this monitoring programme was to inform management. Key metrics focused on changes in:

\begin{itemize}
  \item Indigenous biodiversity
  \item Plant composition (especially threatened species) 
  \item Ground cover
\end{itemize}

The 30 transects which make up the monitoring programe consist of two types: (1)  ten-closely spaced transects restricted to a recreational reserve, and (2) twenty more widely-spaced transects found across conservation covenant.

\section*{Purpose of current analysis}

It is hypothesised that the current grazing regime is insufficient to regulate the crowding out of native herbs by exotic grasses at the site. Going forward it was agreed that a two stage analysis of the data would take place:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Stage 1:} Would consist of a series of retrospective analyses to understand the trajectories at the site in respect of the three metrics previously identified. The deliverables were to include a short report that would inform a discussion with Richard Clayton and Ellen Cieraad regarding on the direction of the next stage.
  \item \textbf{Stage 2:} Based on the decisions undertaken at the end of Stage 1, the signals present in the data, and operational constraints, a series of statistical tools would be employed to identify the design parameters required to detect the effectiveness future management. At the time of writing it is unclear whether what type of inferential study would be likely to be employed (e.g. causal inference, a BACI-like study, or a before-after intervention).
\end{itemize}




\section*{Exploratory analysis}

I began my analysis began with some simple proof of concept analyses involving indigenous biodiversity and sequentially introducing complexity. Such an approach ensures that any mistakes or misunderstanding in purpose, or underlying data can be quickly identified and corrected. Furthermore, such an approach allows us to identify any design and convergence issues which will likely affect the wider suite of metrics.


\subsection*{Indigenous biodiversity}

Initial exploratory modelling focused on indigenous biodiversity due to its high tangibility and suggested the area is becoming more depauperate over (see Appendix). When more complex analyses were undertaken incorporating spatial elements it was revealed the that while the transects across the convenant area (CA plots) were well dispersed across the fan, the RR plots were tightly clustered within a reserve found on the southern edge of the fan but within the wider CA extent. Such tight spatial clustering typically presents a problem in terms of sampling bias and raises issues of spatial auto-correlation. Initial models\footnote{Upon suggestion from Ellen Cieraad I implemented a series of models which examined whether the response variable was associated with a decaying distance relationship with neighbouring observations i.e. via a \emph{exp(pos + 0 $\mid$ spatial\_group)}.} failed to converge leading me to suspect there might be a directional gradient underpinning the random errors. Formal tests of the random errors via Moran's I test revealed that there was some form of spatial auto-correlation. 

Plotting the random errors associated with the top-ranked model suggested a spatial relationship running perpendicular to the Ashley River (Figure \ref{fig:spatial.signal}). I then tested Richard Clayton's suggestion that the relationship might be better described by the relationship with the fence line. Indeed, modelling with both terms revealed that there was likely some gradient influencing the biodiversity response associated with distance from the fence line (Figure \ref{fig:signal.corrected}). Inclusion of this term resulted in the random errors associated with plot diminishing substantially, and a new top-ranked model being established. 

The predictions from the new top-ranked model suggest that samples found closer to the fence line have higher indigenous biodiversity but more likely to loose indigenous species at a faster rate than those located further away (Figure \ref{fig:distance.fence}).


