\subsection*{Bareground cover}

While other cover classes on the fan appear to be stable it is evident that  bare ground appears to be disappearing (Figure \ref{fig:bare} \& \ref{fig:bgcover}).

A hurdle model was used to assess changes in bare ground cover (logistic regression to assess presence-absence followed by a beta binomial regression to determine proportion of bare ground). The presence of bare ground was associated with  year (top ranked model: \emph{as.factor(Year) + (1 $\mid$ Plot/Subplot)}, AICc weight = 0.73) with bare ground diminishing over time (from an average of 18.2\% per subplot in 2018 to 2.6\% in 2026).

Modelling suggest that the proportion of bare ground increased with proximity to the fence but was declining over time (top ranked mode: \emph{Year + scale(fence.dist) + (1 $\mid$ Plot/Subplot)}, AICc weight = 0.43). However this beta regression model had multimodal residuals, suggesting the existence of a potential hidden process (i.e. some semi-predictable factor we have not accounted for is driving a pattern in the amount of bare ground we are recording) . Graphing appeared to confirm that bare ground seems to appear in quantised clumps, suggesting that there may be underlying hidden process in play (Figure \ref{fig:multimodel}). While the existence of such a phenomenon is speculative localised disturbance caused by rabbit scrapes would potentially be one explaination.

