\subsection*{Key species}

The three plant species present on the fan with the highest threat classification were singled out for modelling\footnote{I note that while the fan-leaved mat daisy \emph{Raoulia monroi} (Nationally Vulnerable) has been reported as being present in the valley it was not recorded in the current dataset.}. These were:

\begin{itemize}
  \item Lees Valley Daisy,\emph{Brachyscome pinnata} (Nationally Critical)
  \item Dryland sow thistle \emph{Sonchus novae-zelandiae} (Nationally Vulnerable) 
    \item Limestone aniseed \emph{Gingidia enysii} (Nationally Endangered) 

\end{itemize}

As plant cover was estimated using ordered cover class categories. Cumulative link models with random effects were used to estimate 


\emph{Brachyscome pinnata} appears to be constrained to the southern portion of the fan and appears to be fast disappearing (Figure \ref{fig:brapin}). Intensive modelling revealed that there was no support for subplot random effects and their inclusion was an impediment for robust modelling (causing numerous diagnostic issues).

Preliminary modelling (top-ranked model \emph{Year + scale(fence.dist) + (1 $\mid$ Plot)}, AICc weight = 0.80), suggests the presence of \emph{Brachyscome pinnata} is declining \mytilde 12\% per year. 

There is no evidence that \emph{Sonchus novae-zelandiae} is declining but rather it's presence maybe associated with a gradient extending from the fence (Figure \ref{fig:sonnov}; top-ranked model \emph{scale(fence.dist) + (1 $\mid$ Plot)}, AICc weight = 0.40). However, the second ranked model was a null model thereby suggesting there is effectively no detectable pattern whatsoever (\emph{1 + (1 $\mid$ Plot)}, AICc weight = 0.33).

\emph{Gingidia enysii} has only been found on two transects (once on in a single subplot on R10 and in five subplots on RR3). Modelling was restricted to RR3 and limited to null model and a year effect. The top-ranked model (\emph{1 + (1|Subplot)}) suggested the presence of \emph{G. enysii} was best explained as a simple average(AIC weight = 0.66), while the second-ranked model (\emph{Year  + (1|Subplot)}  suggested that the probablity of finding the species was declining year on year (AIC weight = 0.34). However, the species when present was found to be proportionally increasing year on year (\emph{Year + (1 | Subplot)}) due to a proliferation at a single subplot (i.e. RR3 Subplot 960).


