\appendix

\section*{Appendix}

\subsection*{Initial biodiversity analysis}

When indigenous biodiversity is only assessed at the plot level a pattern (top-ranked model: \mytilde \emph{Year * Exotic + (1 $\mid$ Plot)}, AIC weight = 0.74) emerges in which indigenous biodiversity appears to reduce over time along with exotic biodiversity (Figure \ref{fig:simpl.biodiversity}). This model was found to have good predictive agreement; thereby suggesting the community might be becoming more depauperate over time. However, upon discussion the pattern was regarded as potentially spurious due to the sensitivity of the plot-level metric to chance, non-representative species occurrences. Further discussion led to the request to retain the nested design (i.e. 1 $\mid$ Plot/Subplot) regardless if the random effects for a particular metric were supported in each particular case. 

During the exploratory process I identified that there was meaningful underdispersion (i.e the variance in biodiversity results was less than expected), use of the generalised Poisson distribution family improved diagnostics but only use of a Conway-Maxwell Poisson distribution settled them to a desirable level. However, the Conway-Maxwell Poisson distribution proved to have a massive impact on computability (with inclusion of subplot level random effects causing such slowness it effectively negated any practical usage). Given that the coefficients produced by generalised Poisson and Conway-Maxwell Poisson distributions were generally very similar to each other I was advised to use to the generalised Poisson.


